    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that the sum of the numbers shown on two standard 6-sided dice is a prime number, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify all possible outcomes**: Each die has 6 faces, so there are \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\) possible outcomes when two dice are tossed.\n\n2. **Calculate the total number of favorable outcomes**: We need to count the number of outcomes where the sum of the numbers on the dice is a prime number.\n\n3. **List the prime numbers that can be the sum of two dice**: The possible sums of two dice range from 2 to 12. The prime numbers in this range are 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11.\n\n4. **Count the number of ways to achieve each prime sum**:\n    - **Sum = 2**: (1, 1) — 1 way\n    - **Sum = 3**: (1, 2), (2, 1) — 2 ways\n    - **Sum = 5**: (1, 4), (2, 3), (3, 2), (4, 1) — 4 ways\n    - **Sum = 7**: (1, 6), (2, 5), (3, 4), (4, 3), (5, 2), (6, 1) — 6 ways\n    - **Sum = 11**: (5, 6), (6, 5) — 2 ways\n\n5. **Sum the favorable outcomes**: Add the number of ways to get each prime sum.\n    \\[\n    1 + 2 + 4 + 6 + 2 = 15\n    \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the probability**: The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes.\n    \\[\n    \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of possible outcomes}} = \\frac{15}{36}\n    \\]\n\n7. **Simplify the fraction**: The fraction \\(\\frac{15}{36}\\) can be simplified by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 15 and 36, which is 3.\n    \\[\n    \\frac{15}{36} = \\frac{15 \\div 3}{36 \\div 3} = \\frac{5}{12}\n    \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the sum of the numbers shown on the dice is a prime number is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{5}{12}}\\).",
